<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Osroene</h1><p class="st-meta">132 BCE – 244 CE (approx.) · capital Edessa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Osroene, the kingdom of Edessa in upper Mesopotamia, cradle of Syriac literature and one of the earliest centres of Christianity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> lost its eastern lands, a local dynasty of Arab origin took power at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-edessa">Edessa</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Şanlıurfa</a>, c. 132 BCE. Its kings, many called Abgar or Ma'nu, ruled a small kingdom between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a>, balancing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-edessa">Edessa</a> grew rich on the trade crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. Its kings wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> dialect that became a great literary language. A legend told of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abgar-v">Abgar V</a> corresponding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread early; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abgar-viii">Abgar VIII</a> is often said to have converted, though this is debated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> deposed the king in 214 CE and made Osroene a Roman colony. The dynasty was briefly restored but ended under Abgar X in the 240s, and the kingdom became a Roman province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/osroene.jpg" alt="Osroene" width="320" height="317"><figcaption>Image: www.armenica.org. The original English map was uploaded by Nareklm · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Armenian_Empire_of_Tigranes_(English).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>132 BCE – 244 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Edessa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abgar V, Abgar VIII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Syrian religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Syriac</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey, Syria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Edessa, Kingdom of Osroene</dd></dl></aside></div>
